<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacobite rising of 1745</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1745">1745</a>–<a href="/years/1746">1746</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Forty-five, Jacobite rebellion of 1745</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jacobite rising of 1745 was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a>'s attempt to regain the British thrones for his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart">James Francis Edward Stuart</a>. Landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, won at Prestonpans and marched as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> before retreating. The Jacobite army was destroyed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Culloden</a> in April 1746, after which the Highlands were harshly repressed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745.jpg" alt="Jacobite rising of 1745" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Morier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Culloden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688 replaced the Catholic James II and VII with his Protestant daughter Mary and her husband William. James Francis Edward Stuart was the closest natural heir, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a> excluded Catholics, and in 1714 the throne passed to George I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. Jacobite risings in 1715 and 1719 failed, and by 1737 James was living tranquilly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. By the late 1730s French statesmen saw the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">Stuarts</a> as a means of weakening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the Highlands as a place to start. A French invasion planned for February 1744 was wrecked by storms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> cancelled it and declared war on Britain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> launched the rising on 19 August 1745 at Glenfinnan in the Highlands, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and won the Battle of Prestonpans in September. The Jacobite army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 8 November, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> capitulated on 15 November. At Manchester it received its first notable English recruits, who formed the Manchester Regiment. When it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> on 4 December there was no sign of English reinforcements or a French landing, and on 5 December the council voted overwhelmingly to retreat. Charles had admitted that he had not heard from the English Jacobites since leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and his relationship with the Scots was irretrievably damaged.</p><p>The Jacobites won a narrow victory at Falkirk Muir on 17 January 1746 but abandoned the siege of Stirling Castle and retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Battle of Culloden</a> on 16 April, fewer than 5,000 Jacobites faced a better trained government force of 7,000 to 9,000 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a>. The government guns were far superior, and when Charles ordered a charge, boggy ground drove his centre to the right, where the regiments became entangled. The battle lasted less than an hour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Government forces spent weeks searching for rebels, confiscating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and burning meeting houses. Of 3,500 Jacobites indicted for treason, 120 were executed, 650 died awaiting trial, 900 were pardoned and the rest were transported to the colonies. Lords Kilmarnock, Balmerino and Lovat were beheaded, and the remaining prisoners were released under the General Pardon Act 1746. Charles escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1788. The rising was the last of a series that began in 1689 and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobitism">Jacobitism</a> as a major factor in British politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacobite rising of 1745” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Almost all the standing masonry was removed in early modern times for local roads and farmhouses, and in the 18th century General Wade used long sections for the military road built to crush the <b>Jacobite rising of 1745</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>God Save the King appeared close to its present form in 1744 and spread among supporters of George II during the <b>Jacobite rising of 1745</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>When Charles Edward Stuart led the <b>Jacobite rising of 1745</b>, the song spread among supporters of King George II; it was sung at the London playhouses of Drury Lane and Covent Garden, Thomas Arne wrote a setting for Drury Lane, and The Gentleman's Magazine printed it in October…</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a></li><li><blockquote>Wade, an English Catholic layman, lived in exile in France, where he had fled after the <b>Jacobite rising of 1745</b> was crushed, and made his living copying musical manuscripts he found in libraries.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a></li><li><blockquote>It has often been dated to 1746, and its origins are thought to lie in the <b>Jacobite rising of 1745</b>, with which it has long been associated.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a></li></ul></section>
